---
import ExecutionDemo from '../ExecutionDemo.astro';
---

<section id="tool" class="section" aria-labelledby="tool-title">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2 id="tool-title">03 Harness with Policy</h2>
    <div class="report-prose">
      <p>
        Invoking a post-trained policy raises SR from 46.7% under direct control to 53.0% with π₀.₅
        and to 56.0% with InternW0-Δ, mainly on long-horizon and tabletop tasks. The harness with
        InternW0-Δ thereby surpasses OpenWAM-α, the strongest post-trained policy, on both metrics.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul
      class="highlight-grid highlight-grid--compact"
      aria-label="Direct control against the harness with π₀.₅ and with InternW0-Δ"
    >
      <li>
        <strong>46.7 → 53.0 / 56.0</strong>
        <span>SR (%) with π₀.₅ / InternW0-Δ</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>8.1M → 3.5M / 4.0M</strong>
        <span>tokens per success</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>42.6 → 23.7 / 22.7</strong>
        <span>tool calls per episode</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="paper-figure" id="fig-harness-radar">
      <img
        src="media/figures/harness_radar.svg"
        width="406"
        height="367"
        alt="Radar charts of SR for direct control, each policy alone, and the harness with that policy"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        SR of the agent under direct control, of each policy alone, and of the harness with that
        policy, for π₀.₅ in (a, b) and InternW0-Δ in (c, d), across task groups in (a, c) and across
        generalization dimensions in (b, d).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ExecutionDemo
      id="harness-episodes"
      title="How the agent and the policy share the work"
      catalog="data/episodes/harness.json"
    >
      <p>
        The bar under each recording marks the executor: the agent's own commands in blue, π₀.₅ in
        purple, and InternW0-Δ in teal. In the two successes, the policy completes the bimanual and
        articulated stages. The three failures show where the coordination stays loose: a completion
        claim on an unseated gear, a grasp left unmaintained after a policy segment, and a policy
        segment that undoes a completed stage.
      </p>
    </ExecutionDemo>
  </div>
</section>
